<div class="intro">
    <markdown>
        # 12 Days of Htmx-mas

        [Htmx](https://htmx.org) lets you build modern, interactive web apps with plain HTML attributes. There is no build step and
        no JavaScript framework to wrangle: the server renders HTML, and Htmx swaps it into the page. It gets even better
        when the server is [ASP.NET Core](https://asp.net), because the
        [Razor](https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/mvc/views/razor) templating engine makes reusable HTML partials
        and C# TagHelpers a breeze. Don't take our word for it: this very website is built with Htmx and Razor!

        Open a door below to try all 12 samples. Each one tackles a scenario you will run into in your own web
        applications, from counters and forms to infinite scroll and keyboard shortcuts. Work through all 12 days and
        you'll be ready to build something wonderful with Htmx.

        ## Getting Started with Htmx

        To install Htmx, add the following script to your web application:

        ```html
        <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/htmx.org@4.0.1/dist/htmx.min.js"></script>
        ```

        Learn more on the [official website](https://htmx.org). You can also download the script and serve it
        from your own app.

        Optionally, add the Htmx.Net libraries to make Htmx more enjoyable to use from .NET. Htmx.Net 2.0 is
        currently a preview release, so include the `--prerelease` flag:

        ```console
        dotnet add package Htmx --prerelease
        dotnet add package Htmx.TagHelpers --prerelease
        ```

        This solution uses those NuGet packages, which makes authoring Htmx applications a lot more pleasant.

        Enjoy, and happy Htmx-mas!
    </markdown>
</div>

